        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Pressure-treated lumber takes stain perfectly well, and almost every treated deck, fence and pergola looks and lasts better with a finish on it. What it will not do is take stain on the day it comes home from the lumberyard. Most treated wood leaves the plant soaked with the water that carried the preservative into it, and until enough of that water leaves, any stain you apply has nowhere to go. The calendar rules you see quoted, 30 days or 60 or six months, are all attempts to guess how long that drying takes. A two-minute test on the wood itself replaces the guesswork, and the rest of this page explains how to run it, how to read it and what to do once the wood says yes.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            You can stain pressure-treated wood, and outdoors you generally should. The preservative in treated lumber protects it from rot fungi and termites. It does nothing against sunlight, which grays and roughens the surface, or against the repeated wetting and drying that causes cracking, checking and cupping. A stain or water-repellent finish handles those, and the combination of treatment plus finish is what gives a deck or fence a long, good-looking life.
          </p>

          <p>
            The condition is that the wood must be dry enough to absorb the finish. Most standard treated lumber is sold wet, and stain applied to wet wood cannot soak in. It sits on the surface, dries slowly, and either wears away within months or, if it forms a film, peels as moisture leaves the wood underneath it. That failure is so common that many people conclude treated wood cannot be stained at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is to let the wood tell you when it is ready rather than following a fixed number of days. Sprinkle a little water on the surface. If it beads and sits there, wait. If it darkens the wood and soaks in within several minutes, the wood is ready for stain. Everything else on this page is detail around that one test.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two exceptions shorten the wait. Lumber labeled KDAT, for kiln-dried after treatment, has already had most of the water driven out and can often be finished soon after installation. And old, weathered treated wood that has been outside for a year or more is long since dry; it simply needs cleaning before it is stained.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ANSWER IN ONE LINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Stain it when water soaks in, not when the calendar says so.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Sprinkle water on several boards. Beading means the wood is still wet from treatment; absorption within several minutes means it is ready. Test sunny and shaded boards separately, because they dry at different speeds.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="why-wet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why New Treated Lumber Refuses Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure treatment is a wet process. Lumber, usually southern yellow pine or another absorbent softwood, is loaded into a sealed cylinder, and a water-based preservative solution is forced deep into the wood under pressure. Today's residential treatments are copper-based formulas such as ACQ, copper azole and micronized copper azole. The older CCA treatment, which contained arsenic, was phased out for most residential uses in the early 2000s.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the preservative is in, most lumber is wrapped and shipped without being dried again. Boards often arrive at the store heavy and cool to the touch, and some are visibly damp. Their cells are full of water, so there is no room for a stain to enter. A finish needs empty, open wood fibers to soak into, and saturated wood has none.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treated pine can also have a smooth, compressed surface from the planer, sometimes called mill glaze, and occasionally a light residue from the treatment. These slow absorption further. They wear away naturally after some weeks of weather, and a cleaning and light brightening before staining removes most of what remains.
          </p>

          <p>
            As the lumber dries outdoors, it shrinks. Deck boards installed tight together often open into gaps, and some boards cup, twist or check. That movement is another reason to let the wood settle before finishing: a stain applied before the wood has finished its first round of shrinking can crack along new checks and leave unfinished wood exposed.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means the wood is defective. Wet treated lumber is normal and expected. It just means the finishing step belongs a little later in the project than most people plan for, and it is worth building that gap into your schedule from the start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Two-Minute Test, Done Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use a spray bottle, a cup or your fingertips to put a few tablespoons of water on the face of a board. Watch for several minutes. If the water stays in beads or a puddle with sharp edges and the wood around it does not darken, the board is still too wet or too sealed to accept stain. If the water darkens the wood and disappears into it within several minutes, the board is ready.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test more than one board. Decking in full sun dries far faster than boards in the shade of the house, and the railings and stair stringers dry at their own pace. Thick posts and beams hold water longest. Test at least five or six spots across the project, including the shadiest and the sunniest, and go by the slowest result for whatever you plan to stain that day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test on a dry day. Morning dew, a recent rain or a hose-down will make any wood bead or soak in unpredictably. Wait until the surface has been dry for at least a day, ideally two, and run the test in the afternoon when the boards have had sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pin-type moisture meter adds a number to the answer. Push the pins into the board across the grain and read the moisture content. Many stain manufacturers ask for wood below roughly 12 to 15 percent; wet treated lumber can read well above 20 percent, and sometimes above the top of a cheap meter's range. A meter is worth its modest price if you are building a large deck.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
