        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Treated lumber is worth staining, as long as you wait until it has dried enough to absorb the finish. Skip the calendar rules, sprinkle water on several boards, and stain when it soaks in. KDAT lumber is often ready within days; standard lumber may need weeks or months depending on climate and season.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When the wood is ready, clean it, brighten if needed, test the color and apply a semi-transparent penetrating stain in thin coats. Treat every cut end during construction, handle the lumber with basic precautions and never burn the scraps. For product picks, see our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

